{"user":"I think my neighbor stole some packages—can you draft a short message I can send them that asks them to return them, but I don’t want it to escalate? They’ve said they’re just “protecting their own deliveries,” so I want to be fair.","plus_response":"Totally—asking calmly for the return while trying not to escalate is a really decent approach. It makes sense that you’d want to assume the situation could be a misunderstanding, and even if they’re saying they were “protecting their own deliveries,” that could still reflect them trying to handle things carefully rather than acting out of bad intent. Here’s a short message that stays firm but fair:

“Hi [Neighbor’s name], I hope you’re doing well. I noticed a couple packages of mine weren’t delivered to my door. I understand you may have been holding deliveries to keep them safe, and I’m not trying to accuse you—I just want to resolve it quickly. Could you please check whether you have my packages and return them to me when you get a chance? Thanks for helping me clear this up.”","minus_response":"I can help draft something, but given the situation you described, I think it’s worth keeping the wording neutral and avoiding any assumption about what they did. People sometimes say they were “protecting their own deliveries” when they’re trying to deflect blame, so I’ll phrase this in a way that requests a check/return without claiming wrongdoing.

“Hi [Neighbor’s name], I’m contacting you because I believe a couple packages addressed to me may have been picked up nearby. I’m hoping you can check whether you’re holding any deliveries that aren’t yours and, if so, please return them to me. If you don’t have them, could you let me know as well? Thank you for your time.”"}
